%T A401110 17,33,28,17,1,5,0,3,23,29,52,39,21,3,6,0,4,14,31,61,84,50,32,4,6,0,0,
%U A401110 14,22,61,92,111,78,33,2,7,0,0,5,27,46,111,132,185,97,29,4
%N A401110 Triangle read by rows: T(n, k) is the number of inequivalent convex lattice polygons with n lattice points, k of them interior, 0 <= k <= n - 3.
%C A401110 Two polygons are equivalent under affine unimodular transformations x -> A*x + b, where A is in GL_2(Z) and b is in Z^2. Reflections are allowed.
%C A401110 The row sums are A371917; the column sums for k >= 1 are A322343. By Pick's theorem, the antidiagonal sum for fixed n + k = s is A187015(s - 2).
%C A401110 The formulas for k = 0 and k = 1 follow from Koelman's classification (Castryck, Theorem 10(a, b)) and the duality of the 16 reflexive polygons (Poonen and Rodriguez-Villegas, Theorem 1).
%C A401110 Scott's theorem gives the vanishing formulas below. The upper-diagonal formulas follow from Koelman's classification of polygons with collinear interior lattice points (Castryck, Theorem 10(c)) and the bound of Haase and Schicho for all other polygons (Lemma 11).
%C A401110 The right edge T(n, n - 3) counts clean lattice triangles, whose only boundary lattice points are their vertices. Its formula is equivalent to the counting result of Khan and Khan (Theorem 17; see link).
%C A401110 Proofs and further details are given in the linked note.
%H A401110 Felix Huber, <a href="/A401110/b401110.txt">Rows n = 3 .. 47, flattened</a>.
%H A401110 Wouter Castryck, <a href="https://homes.esat.kuleuven.be/~wcastryc/movingout.pdf">Moving out the edges of a lattice polygon</a>, Discrete Comput. Geom. 47 (2012), 496-518.
%H A401110 Christian Haase and Josef Schicho, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0406224">Lattice polygons and the number 2i + 7</a>, arXiv:math/0406224 [math.CO], 2004-2007; Amer. Math. Monthly 116 (2009), no. 2, 151-165.
%H A401110 Felix Huber, <a href="/A401110/a401110.pdf">Illustration of row 6</a>.
%H A401110 Felix Huber, <a href="/A401110/a401110.txt">Maple program</a>.
%H A401110 Felix Huber, <a href="/A401110/a401110_2.pdf">Proofs and formulas for the lattice polygon triangle</a>.
%H A401110 Mizan R. Khan and Riaz R. Khan, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2012.11081">To count clean triangles we count on imph(n)</a>, arXiv:2012.11081 [math.CO], 2020.
%H A401110 Bjorn Poonen and Fernando Rodriguez-Villegas, <a href="https://math.mit.edu/~poonen/papers/lattice12.pdf">Lattice polygons and the number 12</a>, Amer. Math. Monthly 107 (2000), no. 3, 238-250.
%H A401110 Paul R. Scott, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1017/S0004972700022826">On convex lattice polygons</a>, Bull. Austral. Math. Soc. 15 (1976), 395-399.
%H A401110 Eric Weisstein's World of Mathematics, <a href="https://mathworld.wolfram.com/PicksTheorem.html">Pick's Theorem</a>.
%F A401110 T(n, 0) = floor(n/2) for n >= 3, n != 6; T(6, 0) = 4.
%F A401110 T(n, 1) = T(14 - n, 1) for 4 <= n <= 10.
%F A401110 T(n, k) = 0 for n > 3*k + 6 if k >= 2, and for n > 10 if k = 1.
%F A401110 T(3*k + 6, k) = k + 2 for k >= 4.
%F A401110 T(3*k + 5, k) = k + 1 for k >= 4.
%F A401110 T(3*k + 2, k) = 5*k + 1 for k >= 7.
%F A401110 T(3*k + 6 - 2*m, k) = (m + 2)*(k - m + 2) for m >= 1, m != 2, k >= 2*m + 3.
%F A401110 T(3*k + 5 - 2*m, k) = (m + 2)*(k - m + 1) + 1 for m >= 2, k >= 2*m + 4; also for m = 1, k >= 7.
%F A401110 T(n, n - 3) = (A058026(2*n - 5) + 3 + 2*A000086(2*n - 5))/6.
